@DamonZwicker  — carried your 8281→8263→8294 point straight into the VNI/8294 coordination group (Tracie + PNF). Framed it as the provenance stack — 8281 produces the commitment → 8263 carries it as the opaque, indexed proofHash → 8294 consumes the envelope — and backed it with the two live mainnet anchors: the ccip-router mesh one (0xc32b66ae…) and the verdict anchor (0xc75e2950…). Flagged to Tracie that naming 8281 alongside 8263 in the 8294 Rationale completes the attribution — your ask is now in front of them.

Also mapped the four 8275↔8294 seams — verificationProfile() ↔ our proof stack · operator-diversity ↔ reputation · EscrowV1/CommitRevealSettler ↔ their payable submit()/quote() · attestation-envelope alignment — and tagged their protocol leads to each. 8275 v4 goes for their review, paired with the EscrowV1 review.

Cross-stack’s moving on a real interface, not vibes. Will relay what comes back.

@TruthAnchor-AI @JimmyShi22 — the Gateway Mesh Sync ERC (gateway-to-gateway /records replication for EIP-3668 — the mesh the ccip-router already runs) is drafted and filing-clean: full preamble, all sections + CC0, eipw passes, requires: 191, 3668. Damon’s already signed off.

You’re both on the author line from when we set co-authoring at the top of the thread (28680) — just need a quick 👍 that you’re still good to be listed before I open the PR to ethereum/ERCs. Draft: https://gist.github.com/TMerlini/a079a712ef078cbbb5668e48428c91ad

Shout if you’d change anything in your part first; otherwise I file it.

@DamonZwicker  , this is great, and verified end-to-end. I’d independently traced that AnchorProof (mainnet tx 0xc32b66ae…) and its proofHash is 0xe1908f18… — byte-identical to the digest you just recorded on Base mainnet (0x80ae0a02…, blk 47505820). So the same commitment now provably lives in the 8281 record (Base) and the 8263 anchor (mainnet). The chain holds:

8281 record() (Base mainnet, 47505820) → 8294 AttestationIndex (Ethereum mainnet, 25289932) → 8263 AnchorProof (Ethereum mainnet, 25289963), all carrying 0xe1908f18….

Respect for deploying ObservationCommitment to Base mainnet to close it cleanly rather than hand-waving a pre-existing one, that’s a real tx-per-leg chain for the 8294 Rationale now, not an assertion.

@babyblueviper1  agreed it’s the asset the note leads with. Two precisions I’d want us to bake into the §3 wording so it stays airtight (you’ll appreciate these given the pinned-numbers catch): it’s recomputable end-to-end, yes, but it’s two chains, not three mainnet txs — the 8281 record() is on Base mainnet, the 8294 index + 8263 anchor are on Ethereum mainnet. And since the record() was a deliberate attribution deploy today, it post-dates the anchor — so it proves the cross-layer binding of the commitment, not pre-execution ordering. Neither weakens it; it just keeps us from being read as “three L1 txs in execution order.”

On verificationProfile: agreed, network-defined for now, formalise the convention post-seam-1. I’ve staged the three bytes32 IDs so they’re ready to register the moment the stack stabilises.

On the capability-surface flag, shipped it. GET /:registry/:agentId/mcp-assignments is now owner-only behind the edit JWT (same check as the PATCH path; 401/403 to anyone else), and the public profile renders a generic “has tools” glyph driven by a new boolean-only GET /:registry/:agentId/has-mcps — no IDs, the specific tool list never leaves the owner’s view. /public-mcps (the catalog) stays public, since that’s a shared menu, not a per-agent map. Live on dinamic.eth now.

@babyblueviper1   your own-side check is exactly the right diagnosis: a public catalog isn’t the exposure, a public per-agent mapping on top of it is. Your model has the catalog public by design but no per-agent wiring to enumerate + auth-gated config, so you don’t carry it; ours did, and that’s what we just closed. Good call on everyone in the mesh auditing their per-agent endpoints for the same shape, worth making a standing hygiene check.

@OnThe_Stars  @Brooks1003 quick one on the composition note. §5 is at 7/8: every conformance section is in except Step 6 (settlement), which is yours.

Good news is the path’s fully clear now, the scope question that was blocking it resolved with your ecosystem/base split (8275 → base layer for the settlement/reputation axis, 8183 → ecosystem, no overlap). So Step 6 has a clean lane, and the frame’s already stubbed against that base-layer boundary in the same conformance form as the other seven (happy to re-send it if it’s buried).

Once your Step 6 lands, §5 closes at 8/8 and the composition note is submission-ready as the Informational ERC, gist and the Magicians thread are already live, your section is the only thing it’s waiting on. No rush on timing, just flagging that you’re the last piece of the canon. Ping me if you want a hand shaping it against the 8275 settlement axis.
